In article <1991Aug19.203019.4288@neon.Stanford.EDU> mk@neon.Stanford.EDU (Mike Killianey) writes:
>I was talking with Nelson Lu about NetHack memories, and we were wondering
>about other people's games...what's the longest game you've ever played?
>What's the shortest game you've actually played (wins, only, of course)?
>
>By longest, I mean game hours, not "I started in May and remembered two
>years later that I forgot to finish, and I did!"  Although the long-
>forgotten games certainly could be interesting, too...

Game hours is kind of hard to quantify, but fortunately, nethack has
the "time" environment variable to show you exactly how many moves the
game took.  If set, the number of moves will be displayed on the
bottom stat line.  Since whenever I win the game, I take a snapshot of
my final stats, etc, and save them in a file, I was able to look up my
longest game. It took 131181 moves, and was the result of one of those
bring-five-figures-worth-of-dilithium-crystals-to-the-endgame types.
Assuming one move per second, though, the game would have taken 3 days
playing 12 hr/dy, although in acutality, the game was played over a
period of 2.5 weeks. My shortest winning game was about half that
long, although I'm sure you can find other hackers out there that have
had much longer/shorter games than this.

>Also, is there a theoretical minimum number of turns for a winning game?
>Does the code enforce a maximum?

From my knowledge, there is no limit on the maximum number of turns a
game can take, although certain things may happen after an extreme
length of time (e.g. everything gets auto-genocided, etc). The
absolute theoretical minimum number of turns for a win I'd estimate to
be about 1000 moves based on the following scenario: Find 5 wands of
wishing in the room you start in (as I was saying, this is clearly
theoretical!), wish for armor, weapons, gain level potions, reverse
genocide nurses to get enough hp, teleport control ring, cursed
teleport scroll; level teleport to dlvl 30 where the wiz happens to
be, kill him, take amulet, (he doesn't reincarnate), then go up level
by level, with each up/down stair being only a room apart; on endgame,
polymorph into Xorn and run across level in 80 moves to altar and
offer amulet.

